Both forms, executed:

```go
// Iterative — O(n) time, O(1) space
func fib(n int) int {
    if n <= 1 { return n }
    a, b := 0, 1
    for i := 2; i <= n; i++ {
        a, b = b, a+b
    }
    return b
}
// fib(0..7) → 0 1 1 2 3 5 8 13 — verified

// Naive recursive — O(2^n)
var fibRec func(n int) int
fibRec = func(n int) int {
    if n <= 1 { return n }
    return fibRec(n-1) + fibRec(n-2)
}
// fibRec(10) → 55 — verified (fast at small n; crawls at 40+)
```

The naive recursion's problem: fibRec(n-1) and fibRec(n-2) each recompute the ENTIRE subtree below them — fib(5) computes fib(2) three times, fib(3) twice. The tree doubles every level: fib(40) is ~10^12 calls.

The iterative solution's mechanics: two variables (a = fib(i-2), b = fib(i-1)); each loop advances one step. The simultaneous assignment (a, b = b, a+b) is Go's clean swap: both RHS values evaluated before assignment.

The memoized recursion (the interview middle ground): store computed values in a map — the recursive shape, O(n) time:
```go
var memo = map[int]int{0: 0, 1: 1}
func fibMemo(n int) int {
    if v, ok := memo[n]; ok { return v }
    v := fibMemo(n-1) + fibMemo(n-2)
    memo[n] = v
    return v
}
```

The interview flow: write iterative (the production answer), explain why naive recursion is exponential, show memoization as the technique for 'recursive shape + iterative cost.'
